{"verdict": "accept", "reason": "On Debian, `password_lock: true` makes the Ansible user module run `usermod -L service`, which puts '!' in front of the stored hash so password login stops working. Baseline (unlocked hash): the hash becomes '!<hash>', so the password is disabled. Adversarial (hash already locked): the module sees the leading '!' and skips the change, so the password stays disabled. Both end states meet the request, and the request does not ask for the hash to be removed or replaced."}
```

